// t_80dad2dd: a value the half colour SHARES with another consumer.
//
// The two folds that pin a colour's producers to the output slot also, for
// a half colour, stamp those producers fp16 - which changes the precision
// of the VALUE, not just of the colour written from it.  Here the same
// composed value is also the depth export, so stamping the producers
// exported depth from H0.x: a full-precision output reading a half-rounded
// number, accepted with no diagnostic.
//
// The reference keeps the value in fp32 and converts AT the colour store:
//
//     MOVR R1.xyz, f[TEX0];
//     MOVH H0, R1.xyzz;
//     MOVR R1.z, R1.x;
//
// so exactly ONE instruction is half - the colour store - and the depth
// reads full precision.  That is the property the test asserts, and it is
// what a shared value falling through to the explicit store produces.
//
// Found by codex reviewing 3618daf8; every guard fixture in place at the
// time passed, because each of them had a colour that owned its value.
void main(float4 p : TEXCOORD0, out half4 c : COLOR, out float d : DEPTH)
{
	float4 v = p;
	v.w = p.z;
	c = v;
	d = v.x;
}
